gm-method (the FASTEST + simplest exact terrain collider on a GPU-fractal planet: bake GPU tiles straight into the physics engine, store nothing). MEASURED: a cube-sphere renderer's batch height-bake (mapspinner bakeTileReadback: one synchronous gl.drawArrays(_HEIGHTBAKE_)+gl.readPixels of a 130^2 R32F tile) costs ~2ms/tile = ~0.0001ms/sample, ~200-400x faster than the CPU fractal (~19-37us/sample) AND faster than a JS bilinear lookup loop over the same samples. CONSEQUENCE that resolves 'read vs calculate vs store': don't store a CPU heightmap and don't recompute on CPU - have the consumer (even a headless server's Web Worker, which HAS OffscreenCanvas WebGL2) bake the needed patches LIVE off the real GPU shader and feed them DIRECTLY into the physics engine as heightfield bodies (a small resident ring near the player, swapped on patch crossing; any patch on the planet bakeable on demand = whole planet). EXACT by construction (the patch IS composeHeight), no float-parity gap, no separate subsystem. TILE->WORLD CONVENTION (pinned, gap 0.00/texel): texel(ix,iz)->face param p=(ox+ix/(res-1)*L, oy+iz/(res-1)*L); faceLocal=faceWarp(p)=R*tan((p/R)*PI/4); dir=normalize(U*faceLocal_x+V*faceLocal_y+center*R) [U,V,center = the FACE_FRAME cols]; tile.heights[iz*res+ix]=composeHeight(dir). The anchor's face = argmax dot(anchorDir, FACE_FRAME[f].center); its face-UV = ox=(4/PI)R*atan(dot(a,U)/dot(a,center)), oy likewise with V. Tile (ox,oy) is the texel-(0,0) corner (spans [ox..ox+L]). 'Same density as the visual triangles' = pick the tile span L so 130-sample spacing == the render leaf's vertex spacing (or just bake the visible quadtree leaves directly - bakeTileReadback is leaf-keyed).